From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: shuuri-labs <61762328+shuuri-labs@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 22:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm/dpu: drive the sm8250 DSPP IGC and 3D gamut

The sm8250 DSPP carries an inverse gamma (IGC v3.1) and a 17^3 gamut LUT
(v4.2) that mainline never drives. Program both with plain register writes
(no reg DMA) and expose them as a 256-entry DEGAMMA_LUT and the CRTC blob
property DPU_3D_LUT (drm_color_lut entries, red fastest), the same contract
as the sm8550 LUTDMA port, so a compositor can move its shaper and 3D LUT off
the GPU. A DSPP joins the topology only on a modeset, so one is requested
when color management starts or stops; a mixer no longer keeps a stale DSPP
pointer from an earlier state.

The table layouts are not documented and were measured on the RP5 by
comparing the interface CRC of a LUT output with a painted reference: the
IGC tables sit in the DSPP top behind one shared index, in G, B, R order,
with 257 samples at i/256 (the 257th must be written); the gamut takes R in
UPPER and G << 16 | B in LOWER, 12 bits each, with consecutive nodes rotating
through its four tables. An identity 3D LUT gives the bypass CRC exactly. An
IGC output never matches a painted frame bit for bit (neighbouring samples
contribute to a solid input; the cause is not identified).

Neither block has a second bank, so a table rewritten while it is enabled
can show one frame mixing old and new values.
